{"author":{"id":"86dc3c885e4fa466d19699d4a5e74c240f1c9d6b23f4e62ea2d2d8e359bd1711"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1789482267,"nonce":"T9YBqXnZZwZHJ62Ybl27k5mdbMQ=","target":"60b2077d4c32098cbfe96ec17c36ead342e68245ab4a5e7a6b372411445fc0ee","message":"**Overview**\n\nGIC v0.9 turns the existing static generative-graphics language and browser preview into a workshop-ready learning environment. The primary product is a dedicated-window desktop IDE for macOS and Windows, with Linux supported on a best-effort basis. A tutor-less installable PWA provides a secondary offline edition. Both editions share the browser-neutral GIC core, Monaco-based editing, diagnostics, Canvas preview, structured output, file workflows, and exports.\n\nThe release is designed for beginners who should not need to understand terminals, local servers, companion processes, credentials files, or editor extensions. Static sketch creation is the release target. Animation remains a stretch goal and cannot block v0.9.\n\nThe desktop edition may optionally host a Socratic AI tutor based on Pi agent libraries. The tutor supports Codex subscription OAuth and OpenCode API-key authentication, contacts a provider only after a student explicitly submits a question, and remains a soft dependency: every core authoring feature works offline and without authentication.\n\nThe command-line interface remains a supported interface. v0.9 must expose stable `gic check` and `gic run` commands over the same language pipeline used by the IDE.\n\n**Goals**\n\n- Deliver a dependable beginner-facing desktop IDE in a dedicated application window.\n- Require no terminal, server, or companion-process management for normal workshop use.\n- Preserve one browser-neutral source of truth for parsing, analysis, execution, diagnostics, formatting, and language assistance.\n- Provide a tutor-less PWA that remains useful after installation without a network connection.\n- Support real `.gic` open, edit, save, recovery, recent-file, and example workflows.\n- Provide Monaco syntax highlighting, diagnostics, completion, hover, signature help, explicit formatting, and format-on-save.\n- Keep preview execution isolated, cancellable, and protected from runaway programs.\n- Export the exact current successful render as PNG and export an editable, self-contained offline HTML sketch.\n- Offer an optional, constrained Socratic tutor without weakening offline use or exposing editing, shell, or web tools.\n- Preserve transparent local tutor sessions and interoperable support files for Codex and OpenCode.\n- Ship stable `gic check` and `gic run` command contracts with useful diagnostics and exit statuses.\n- Establish automated seams that make UI, shell, export, PWA, CLI, and tutor behavior independently testable.\n\n**User Stories**\n\n1. As a workshop student, I want to launch GIC as one normal desktop application so that I do not need a terminal or local server.\n2. As a macOS student, I want an installable package that opens a dedicated GIC window so that the workshop setup feels like a normal application.\n3. As a Windows student, I want an installable package that opens a dedicated GIC window so that I receive the same core workshop experience.\n4. As a Linux student, I want a best-effort packaged build or documented fallback so that I can participate when the supported environment permits it.\n5. As a student, I want the IDE to remain usable without internet access so that a network failure does not stop sketch work.\n6. As a student, I want to edit GIC in Monaco with GIC syntax highlighting so that source structure is legible.\n7. As a student, I want parse and semantic diagnostics at their source ranges so that I can locate mistakes.\n8. As a student, I want completion suggestions for GIC keywords, built-ins, and currently visible symbols so that I can discover valid language elements.\n9. As a student, I want hover and signature help based on the actual GIC language definitions so that editor guidance matches runtime behavior.\n10. As a student, I want an explicit Format Document action so that I can learn and apply canonical formatting deliberately.\n11. As a student, I want format-on-save enabled by default and configurable so that saved files remain consistent without removing my control.\n12. As a student, I want source execution in a cancellable worker with a timeout so that recursion or expensive sketches cannot freeze the IDE.\n13. As a student, I want the Canvas to update shortly after I stop typing so that I can see quick visual feedback.\n14. As a student, I want failed current source to clear stale Canvas output and disable PNG export so that I do not mistake an older image for the current program.\n15. As a student, I want parse, analysis, runtime, and timeout failures presented as GIC diagnostics without JavaScript stack traces so that errors remain understandable.\n16. As a student, I want structured `print` entries in an Output view so that I can inspect program values without developer tools.\n17. As a student, I want a Problems view with an error badge so that diagnostic state is visible even when I am reading output.\n18. As a student, I want errors to reopen a minimized lower panel and select Problems so that important failures are not hidden.\n19. As a student, I want my manually selected Problems or Output tab preserved while the panel is already visible so that routine reruns do not disrupt me.\n20. As a student, I want resizable editor, preview/output, and tutor panes whose sizes and hidden states persist so that I can arrange the workspace for my screen.\n21. As a student, I want Reset Layout so that I can recover from an unusable pane arrangement.\n22. As a student, I want to work on one `.gic` document at a time with Open, Save, Save As, recent files, a dirty indicator, and discard warnings so that the file model stays simple.\n23. As a student, I want explicit saving rather than silent source-file writes so that I understand when my work reaches disk.\n24. As a student, I want private recovery snapshots so that an application or browser interruption does not destroy unsaved work.\n25. As a student, I want recovery to restore an unsaved working copy rather than silently overwrite a `.gic` file so that recovery cannot corrupt my chosen file.\n26. As a student, I want bundled examples to open as editable unsaved copies that require Save As so that shipped examples remain intact.\n27. As a PWA student, I want upload/open and download/save workflows that work across browsers without relying on persistent filesystem handles.\n28. As a student, I want PNG export at the sketch's native Canvas dimensions so that I can use the rendered result outside GIC.\n29. As a student, I want PNG export enabled only for an exact successful run of the current source so that exported pixels never come from stale output.\n30. As a student, I want a self-contained HTML export that opens directly from `file://`, contains no CDN or network dependency, and includes editable source, Canvas, diagnostics, runtime errors, and output so that I can share a living sketch.\n31. As a recipient of an HTML export, I want the sketch to rerun automatically 100 ms after typing stops, cancel the previous worker, and retain timeout protection so that editing remains responsive and safe.\n32. As a recipient of an HTML export, I want the file to work without Monaco, storage access, or a Reset button so that the artifact stays portable and single-purpose.\n33. As a PWA student, I want the installed app to restart offline with the editor, examples, recovery, preview, diagnostics, and exports available so that it is dependable in a workshop room.\n34. As a PWA student, I want an update downloaded in the background but activated only after I confirm it so that the application does not change during an exercise.\n35. As a command-line user, I want `gic check \u003cfile\u003e` to parse and analyze without executing so that I can validate source deterministically.\n36. As a command-line user, I want `gic run \u003cfile\u003e` to execute through the shared core pipeline so that CLI behavior agrees with the IDE.\n37. As a command-line user, I want stable usage text, diagnostics, and success/failure exit statuses with no raw stack traces so that scripts and teaching material can rely on the commands.\n38. As a desktop student, I want an optional tutor pane to stay visible when offline or unauthenticated, showing status, setup instructions, Retry, and Hide controls, so that availability is understandable rather than mysterious.\n39. As a student, I want provider contact only after I explicitly submit a question so that editing and previewing never send source automatically.\n40. As a student asking the tutor, I want the request to include the current source, diagnostics, current runtime error, and structured output so that the tutor can discuss what I see.\n41. As a student, I want the tutor to exclude Canvas images so that text-only models remain supported and image data is not uploaded.\n42. As a student, I want tutor guidance to be Socratic, concise, grounded in bundled GIC documentation/examples, and focused on questions and next steps so that I still do the assignment work.\n43. As an instructor, I want the tutor to refuse complete assignment solutions, TODO completion, source replacement, or generated full functions so that it supports learning rather than substitution.\n44. As an instructor, I want the integrated tutor to have no write, shell, or web tools so that prose policy is reinforced by capability boundaries.\n45. As a student, I want short tutor examples drawn only from shipped GIC reference material so that examples match the course language and remain limited in scope.\n46. As a student, I want tutor responses to be non-selectable and code-copy controls disabled by default, with an accessibility setting that restores normal selection and copying, so that learning mode does not exclude students who need standard interaction.\n47. As a student, I want local tutor sessions with names, start dates, related sketch files, provider/model changes, and conversation entries so that I can resume earlier discussions.\n48. As a student, I want the first question to become the default session name and want to rename it later so that session organization takes little effort.\n49. As a student, I want source and diagnostic context injected per request but not duplicated into session history so that local records stay understandable and avoid unnecessary copies of my work.\n50. As a student, I want large conversations compacted with summary checkpoints while retaining complete transparent history so that the tutor can continue without deleting the record.\n51. As a student, I want renaming a sketch inside GIC to update its session relationship, and continuing a session with another sketch to clone the session, so that the original relationship remains trustworthy.\n52. As a Codex subscriber, I want to authenticate through Codex OAuth and choose from a curated supported model list so that I can use my existing subscription where the packaged runtime supports it.\n53. As an OpenCode user, I want to enter an API key and choose from a curated supported model list so that I can use a configured provider without Codex.\n54. As an advanced student, I want an optional “Enable other models” setting so that I can reveal broader provider catalogs without overwhelming the default experience.\n55. As a student, I want sign-out to remove stored credentials so that I can leave a shared computer cleanly.\n56. As a security-conscious student, I want credentials stored outside the webview in an app-owned protected configuration file with atomic updates and owner-only permissions so that normal application code, exports, and tutor history do not expose them.\n57. As an external Codex or OpenCode user, I want the GIC workspace to contain short always-on guidance and one canonical tutor skill with bundled references so that external sessions follow the same teaching policy.\n58. As a student, I want first-run setup plus Repair/Reinstall and Uninstall actions for external tutor support files so that integration does not require manual file placement.\n59. As a student, I want shipped examples and references to remain read-only sources so that product updates can refresh them safely.\n60. As a student, I want managed support updates to replace only unmodified files, flag modified files, and never overwrite my changes so that customization remains mine.\n\n**Functional Requirements**\n\n1. The shared GIC core must remain independent of DOM, Canvas, Monaco, desktop-shell, and provider APIs.\n2. The language-service surface must provide structured diagnostics, formatting edits or formatted source, completions, hover information, and signature help directly to browser and desktop clients.\n3. A later LSP adapter must be possible without making LSP a v0.9 dependency.\n4. The shared IDE UI must use Monaco and must be reusable by the desktop and PWA editions.\n5. The primary desktop edition must run in a dedicated application window and must not require a separately managed process.\n6. macOS and Windows packages are release requirements; Linux packaging is best-effort.\n7. The initial desktop implementation must evaluate Deno Desktop first and preserve a narrow shell bridge so that only incompatible runtime boundaries need replacement.\n8. Desktop fallback order must be: Deno with Pi modules; Deno with narrowly ported Pi auth/runtime boundaries; Electron; then Tauri with a bundled Node sidecar.\n9. The desktop bridge must expose only required file, configuration, credential, workspace, and application lifecycle operations.\n10. The desktop IDE must support one active document, Open, Save, Save As, recent files, dirty state, discard confirmation, and file rename behavior.\n11. Examples must open from immutable bundled content as unsaved copies.\n12. Desktop and PWA editions must maintain private recovery snapshots without treating snapshots as explicit saves.\n13. The PWA must use portable upload/open and download/save behavior and must not depend on persistent File System Access handles.\n14. Source changes must schedule preview execution after a 100 ms idle period.\n15. Every preview run must use an isolated worker that can be terminated when superseded or timed out.\n16. Worker messages must contain plain structured data.\n17. Only a successful parse, analysis, execution, and render of the current source may update the Canvas and enable PNG export.\n18. Any current-source failure must clear the Canvas and disable PNG export.\n19. PNG export must use native Canvas dimensions.\n20. Standalone HTML export must produce one editable, self-contained, offline file with no network dependency.\n21. Standalone HTML must include the GIC runtime, a preloaded textarea, Canvas, diagnostics, runtime errors, structured output, debounce, worker cancellation, and timeout behavior.\n22. Standalone HTML must not include Monaco, persistent storage, or a Reset button.\n23. Direct `file://` execution of generated standalone HTML is a release contract.\n24. The IDE must provide resizable left editor, middle Canvas plus Problems/Output, and right tutor regions; pane dimensions and hidden states must persist.\n25. Reset Layout must restore the product default arrangement.\n26. If the lower panel is minimized and an error appears, it must reopen with Problems selected.\n27. If the lower panel is visible, reruns must preserve its selected tab while badges update.\n28. The PWA application shell, core, editor support, examples, and export support must be cached for offline restart.\n29. A downloaded PWA update must wait for explicit activation confirmation.\n30. `gic check` must parse and analyze the named `.gic` source without executing it.\n31. `gic run` must execute the named `.gic` source through the same parse, analysis, and runtime pipeline as the IDE.\n32. Both CLI commands must provide documented usage text, stable nonzero failure statuses, source-located diagnostics, and no raw host-language stack traces.\n33. The tutor must be optional and must not gate editing, language assistance, preview, files, examples, recovery, or exports.\n34. The tutor must make no provider request until a student explicitly submits a message.\n35. Each tutor request must receive a current ephemeral context snapshot containing source, diagnostics, runtime failure, and structured output, but no Canvas image.\n36. The integrated agent must use a strong Socratic system prompt, the canonical GIC tutor skill, bundled references, and no write, shell, browser, or web tools.\n37. Tutor policy must prohibit complete solutions, completion of assignment TODOs, wholesale source replacement, and editing tools.\n38. The product must not claim deterministic enforcement of tutor prose or generated-code length.\n39. Tutor response selection and code-block clipboard actions must be disabled by default; one accessibility setting must restore standard selection and copying.\n40. Tutor sessions must use transparent local JSONL records with metadata for name, creation time, related sketch, model/provider changes, conversation entries, and summary checkpoints.\n41. Context snapshots must not be stored as repeated session entries.\n42. Renaming a sketch within GIC must update session relationships; switching a continuing session to a different sketch must clone it and preserve the original.\n43. Codex integration must support subscription OAuth if the packaged runtime spike proves the login, token refresh, streaming, and restart path.\n44. OpenCode integration must support API-key authentication and streamed tutor responses.\n45. Provider selection must begin with curated supported models; a separate advanced setting may reveal broader catalogs.\n46. Credentials must be held outside the webview in a protected app-owned `auth.json`, updated atomically, deleted on sign-out, restricted to mode `0600` on POSIX systems, and protected by an equivalent owner-only ACL on Windows.\n47. Credential contents must never enter webview storage, logs, exports, tutor messages, or session files.\n48. The desktop workspace must present a Processing-style sketches area plus local sessions, root agent guidance, and a canonical tutor skill with bundled references.\n49. External Codex and OpenCode launches must start in the GIC workspace so their project discovery finds the canonical guidance.\n50. First-run setup must install managed external-assistant support; Settings must provide Repair/Reinstall and Uninstall.\n51. Updates may replace unmodified managed files and immutable shipped references, but must flag and preserve every user-modified managed file.\n52. The application must not include a built-in updater before 1.0; releases are installed by replacing the prior package.\n53. Static generative-graphics authoring is the release gate; animation work may ship only if completed without delaying v0.9.\n\n**Non-Goals (Out of Scope)**\n\n- Requiring animation, frame scheduling, or `loop` execution for the v0.9 release.\n- Multi-document tabs, project explorers, imports, package management, or a general-purpose workspace IDE.\n- An LSP server, VS Code extension, or `gic lsp` command.\n- CLI PNG/SVG/GIF export, server rendering, or a CLI web server.\n- A built-in auto-update system before 1.0.\n- Cloud-synchronized GIC accounts, cloud tutor history, or a GIC-operated provider proxy.\n- Canvas screenshots or multimodal tutor context.\n- Proactive tutor hints triggered by editing, diagnostics, or runtime activity.\n- Tutor write, shell, browser, web-search, or source-editing tools.\n- Deterministic validation of every tutor response for pedagogy or code length.\n- Persistent browser filesystem handles as a required PWA capability.\n- Monaco inside standalone HTML exports.\n- Network-loaded fonts, scripts, styles, documentation, or runtime dependencies in offline artifacts.\n- Automatic overwrite of user-modified managed support files.\n- Mobile-native packaging.\n- Linux parity as a v0.9 release blocker.\n\n**Design Considerations**\n\n- The desktop window uses three primary resizable regions: editor on the left, Canvas with a lower Problems/Output panel in the middle, and tutor on the right.\n- The tutor pane remains understandable when unavailable; it does not collapse into an unexplained blank area.\n- Beginner defaults take precedence over exposing every provider or model choice.\n- One active document and explicit save actions keep the mental model close to Processing without introducing project-management complexity.\n- Examples are discoverable but immutable at their source; editing immediately creates an unsaved working copy.\n- Errors are more important than passive output when the lower panel has been hidden, but the application otherwise avoids changing a student's chosen tab.\n- Copy restrictions are a learning-mode default, not an accessibility barrier; the restoring setting must be discoverable and keyboard-operable.\n- The PWA omits the integrated tutor rather than presenting a partially functional or browser-secret-dependent version.\n- Standalone HTML favors portability over IDE features.\n- Desktop and PWA surfaces should share layout and behavior wherever platform capabilities do not require divergence.\n\n**Technical Considerations**\n\n- Current parsing, analysis, interpretation, command recording, structured output, Canvas rendering, and worker timeout behavior provide the baseline rather than being reimplemented.\n- Monaco and all language assistance consume a browser-neutral service; no Node-only LSP dependency belongs in the shared UI.\n- The shell bridge is intentionally narrow because desktop-runtime fallback must not require a UI rewrite.\n- Deno compatibility with Pi streaming and provider/auth helpers is an evidence gate, not an assumption.\n- Pi's browser-compatible agent and provider libraries are preferred; the Node-oriented coding-agent package must not be embedded wholesale into the webview.\n- OAuth is a privileged desktop boundary. Login callback handling, token refresh, secure file updates, restart persistence, and sign-out all require spike evidence in packaged builds.\n- Credential storage deliberately uses a protected app-owned plaintext JSON file rather than an operating-system vault; the webview never receives raw credentials.\n- Worker construction inside direct `file://` HTML differs across browser engines and must be verified in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit before the export contract is accepted.\n- Packaged smoke tests must exercise real built assets rather than only development-server behavior.\n- Tutor tests need a deterministic provider boundary so pedagogy/context/session behavior does not depend on paid or nondeterministic network calls.\n- Real-login smoke checks are still required for each supported provider because fakes cannot validate OAuth, key acceptance, model availability, or streaming protocol compatibility.\n- Session compaction adds checkpoints but never rewrites away the complete transparent JSONL history.\n- External assistant guidance is policy interoperability, not a security sandbox; the integrated tutor's restricted capabilities are stronger than external-tool guarantees.\n\n**Implementation Decisions**\n\n1. Expose a direct browser-neutral language-service API for diagnostics, formatting, completion, hover, and signature help. Defer LSP and VS Code adapters.\n2. Replace the provisional textarea editor with Monaco in the shared IDE UI.\n3. Continue to execute preview work in disposable Web Workers and render plain recorded commands on the UI thread.\n4. Use a shared UI for desktop and PWA editions with small platform adapters for files, recovery, settings, and lifecycle behavior.\n5. Pursue Deno Desktop first. If Pi modules fail, port only the incompatible Pi boundaries before considering Electron; use Tauri with a bundled Node sidecar only after those options fail.\n6. Keep the optional tutor outside the core authoring dependency graph.\n7. Use browser-compatible Pi agent/provider libraries where possible, with a custom GIC session layer and no general coding tools.\n8. Store credentials in a protected app-owned plaintext JSON file outside the webview, with atomic writes and platform-appropriate owner-only access.\n9. Use a curated model list by default and gate broad provider catalogs behind an advanced setting.\n10. Store tutor conversations as transparent local JSONL and inject current editor context ephemerally for each submitted question.\n11. Use one canonical tutor skill and bundled references for the integrated tutor and external Codex/OpenCode support.\n12. Generate standalone HTML as one offline artifact with an embedded runtime and Blob-worker execution where browser evidence permits it.\n13. Preserve `gic check` and `gic run` as narrow CLI entry points over the shared core; do not expand this release into CLI export or server rendering.\n\n**Testing Decisions**\n\n1. Preserve core unit and integration tests for lexer, parser, analyzer, interpreter, recorded commands, structured output, formatting, and language-service results.\n2. Test `gic check` and `gic run` by spawning the real CLI with valid, parse-invalid, analysis-invalid, and runtime-invalid fixtures; assert stdout, stderr, and exit status.\n3. Exercise the shared Monaco IDE through Playwright using user-visible editor, diagnostic, Canvas, output, layout, document, and export behavior rather than test-only DOM markers.\n4. Test the desktop bridge as a narrow contract with temporary real files and configuration directories, then run packaged-application smoke checks on macOS and Windows.\n5. Open every standalone HTML acceptance fixture directly through `file://` in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit; edit source, wait for rerender, verify Canvas/diagnostics/output, and verify cancellation and timeout behavior.\n6. Test tutor context assembly, tool restrictions, Socratic prompt loading, copy restrictions, session persistence, cloning, compaction, provider switching, and credential redaction against deterministic provider implementations.\n7. Run explicit real-login smoke checks for Codex OAuth and OpenCode API-key flows before release; keep credentials out of automated logs and fixtures.\n8. Test the PWA as an installed application: prime it online, restart offline, open examples, edit, recover, render, and export; separately verify that an update waits for confirmation before activation.\n9. Run packaged asset and first-run workspace tests without a development server or pre-existing user configuration.\n10. Test managed-file upgrades with unmodified, modified, missing, and version-skipped files; assert that modified user files are never overwritten.\n11. Keep output pristine: expected failures must be captured and asserted rather than leaking stack traces or unhandled rejection noise.\n12. Treat macOS and Windows package smoke checks as release gates; record Linux results without blocking release.\n\n**Success Metrics**\n\n- A first-time student can install, launch, edit, run, diagnose, save, recover, and export a static `.gic` sketch without using a terminal.\n- The same representative static sketches produce equivalent diagnostics, structured output, and rendered commands in CLI, desktop, PWA, and standalone-export paths where those surfaces overlap.\n- All existing core tests and static browser acceptance tests remain green.\n- Shared-UI Playwright acceptance covers Monaco editing, diagnostics, rendering, output, pane behavior, documents, recovery, examples, and exports.\n- Generated standalone HTML passes direct-`file://` acceptance in Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit.\n- The installed PWA restarts offline and delays update activation until explicit confirmation.\n- Packaged macOS and Windows applications launch without a development server, open/save real `.gic` files, preserve recovery data, and keep credentials outside the webview.\n- `gic check` and `gic run` expose stable documented usage, source-located failures, and deterministic exit statuses.\n- Tutor-disabled, offline, unauthenticated, provider-failure, and sign-out states leave all core IDE features operational.\n- Deterministic tutor tests show that no provider call occurs before submission, no prohibited tools are present, only approved context is attached, and session files omit repeated source snapshots and credentials.\n- Real-login smoke checks demonstrate Codex token refresh and OpenCode key streaming across application restart before the corresponding provider is declared supported.\n- Managed-file upgrade tests prove that user-modified files are preserved and flagged.\n- No animation failure prevents a v0.9 release that satisfies the static acceptance suite.\n\n**Further Notes**\n\n**Required spikes and unresolved questions**\n\n1. **Deno Desktop viability:** Can the selected desktop runtime provide a stable dedicated window, macOS and Windows packaging, native file dialogs, lifecycle hooks, protocol callbacks, and a narrow privileged bridge at the required quality? What supported runtime version is the release baseline?\n2. **Pi under Deno:** Do the browser-compatible Pi agent and provider packages stream correctly in the desktop runtime? Which exact Node assumptions, if any, require narrow ports?\n3. **Codex OAuth:** Can login, callback receipt, token refresh, account switching, restart recovery, and sign-out be completed inside packaged Deno applications without shipping the Node-oriented coding-agent runtime? If not, which fallback boundary is smallest?\n4. **OpenCode contract:** Which OpenCode API endpoint, key format, model-discovery behavior, and streaming protocol are supported for v0.9?\n5. **Model curation:** Which exact default Codex and OpenCode models are offered, what compatibility criteria admit a model, and how are unavailable models communicated?\n6. **CLI `run` presentation:** The command must execute through the shared core, but the user-facing treatment of drawing commands remains to be fixed: headless execution with `print` output only, a textual command representation, or a display backend. This decision must precede its acceptance tests and must not silently add CLI export scope.\n7. **Standalone worker matrix:** Do Blob workers created by a directly opened `file://` document work consistently in current Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit builds? If not, what single-file worker strategy satisfies the same isolation and cancellation contract?\n8. **Signing and installers:** Which signing identities, notarization process, Windows code-signing process, package formats, CPU architectures, and CI runners are available for the workshop release?\n9. **Credential ACLs:** What owner-only Windows ACL implementation is reliable for create, atomic replace, upgrade, and sign-out deletion? How is an insecure existing credential file repaired or rejected?\n10. **Workspace upgrades:** How are modified managed files flagged to students, and does Repair/Reinstall offer a diff, a side-by-side replacement, or only reveal the files in the system file manager?\n11. **Session schema:** Which subset of Pi-compatible JSONL entries is retained, how are GIC metadata and compaction checkpoints represented, and what recovery behavior applies to a partially written final line?\n12. **File relationships:** How are rename, Save As, moved files, deleted files, and PWA download-only documents represented without relying on fragile absolute paths?\n13. **Recovery lifecycle:** When are snapshots created, expired, offered, dismissed, and removed after a confirmed save, and how are multiple application instances handled?\n14. **PWA compatibility baseline:** Which browser versions and operating systems are tested for installation, offline restart, storage quotas, download workflows, and update confirmation?\n15. **External assistant launch:** What reliable cross-platform launch mechanism opens Codex or OpenCode in the workspace, and what fallback instructions are shown when an external application is absent?\n16. **Release accessibility:** Which keyboard navigation, screen-reader announcements, focus restoration, zoom, contrast, and reduced-motion checks form the minimum workshop acceptance set?\n17. **Crash and diagnostic telemetry:** v0.9 assumes local operation and no automatic telemetry. Decide whether opt-in diagnostic export is needed for workshop support without collecting source or credentials.\n\n**Delivery guidance**\n\n- Resolve risky runtime assumptions with disposable spikes before building dependent product slices.\n- Keep every production slice independently testable through one of the agreed seams.\n- Do not turn spike code into production by default; record evidence and implement the smallest supported boundary afterward.\n- Treat user-owned files, source, session history, and credentials as separate data classes with separate lifecycle tests.\n- A later release may promote animation after setup/loop semantics, scheduling, cancellation, and browser rendering pass their own acceptance suite.","files":null}]}